DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     hhoossttaappdd is a daemon which allows communication between different 802.11
     wireless access points running in _H_o_s_t _A_P mode.

     hhoossttaappdd implements the Inter Access Point Protocol (IAPP).  Its purpose
     is to exchange station association updates between access points in large
     wireless networks.  IAPP has been designed to speed up roaming between
     different access points in the same Extended Service Set (ESS).  IAPP is
     described in the IEEE 802.11f standard.

     hhoossttaappdd additionally allows the monitoring and logging of station associ‐
     ations on a non-hostap host which is receiving IAPP messages.

     hhoossttaappdd uses two network interfaces on startup specified in the configu‐
     ration file hostapd.conf(5).  The first interface is used to access the
     Host AP, which is a wireless interface running in Host AP mode.  Host AP
     mode can be enabled using ifconfig(8).  The second interface is used to
     communicate with other hhoossttaappdd in the same broadcast domain or multicast
     group.  Usually a wired interface is used to communicate with other
     hhoossttaappdd.

     hhoossttaappdd broadcasts an _A_D_D_._n_o_t_i_f_y IAPP message when a new station is asso‐
     ciated to the Host AP.  When hhoossttaappdd receives an ADD.notify message, it
     tells the Host AP to remove the specified station.

     hhoossttaappdd may also handle dynamic roaming of IP addresses and routes in ad‐
     dition to the standard IAPP ADD.notify behaviour.  See the section called
     IP Roaming in hostapd.conf(5) for details.

